e) on an elevated stone platform called jangdokdae in the backyard."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-gochujang"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gochujang"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Korean cuisine","cuisine/korean-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":5,"links":[["Chili powder","ingredient/chili-powder"],["Glutinous rice","ingredient/glutinous-rice"],["Gochu-garu","ingredient/gochu-garu"],["Malt","ingredient/malt"],["Meju","ingredient/meju"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Maangchi","ref":"site/maangchi-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"maangchi.com","note":"Maangchi's Korean recipe site and YouTube channel, one of the best known English sources for Korean cooking.","url":"https://www.maangchi.com/","suggested":true},{"name":"Korean Bapsang","ref":"site/koreanbapsang-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"koreanbapsang.com","note":"Home-style Korean recipes from Hyosun, a Korean-American mom.","url":"https://www.koreanbapsang.com/category/recipes/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from Korean cuisine","links":[["Bap (rice dish)","dish/bap-rice-dish"],["Bibim-guksu","dish/bibim-guksu"],["Bibimbap","dish/bibimbap"],["Bosintang","dish/bosintang"],["Cheonggukjang","dish/cheonggukjang"],["Danji","dish/danji"],["Doenjang","dish/doenjang"],["Garak-guksu","dish/garak-guksu"],["Gopchang","dish/gopchang"],["Han Oak","dish/han-oak"],["Hwajeon","dish/hwajeon"],["Jijimi","dish/jijimi"],["Jokbal","dish/jokbal"],["Kim Jong Grillin'","dish/kim-jong-grillin"],["Korean restaurant","dish/korean-restaurant"],["Maekjeok","dish/maekjeok"],["Maeun-tang","dish/maeun-tang"],["Makchang","dish/makchang"],["Mandu-guk","dish/mandu-guk"],["Mandu-gwa","dish/mandu-gwa"],["Maum (restaurant)","dish/maum-restaurant"],["Meat jun","dish/meat-jun"],["Meju","dish/meju"],["Morkovcha","dish/morkovcha"],["Parachute (restaurant)","dish/parachute-restaurant"],["Revelry (restaurant)","dish/revelry-restaurant"],["Scorched rice","dish/scorched-rice"],["Seon (food)","dish/seon-food"],["Sungnyung","dish/sungnyung"],["Toki (restaurant)","dish/toki-restaurant"],["Tteokbokki","dish/tteokbokki"],["Wakame","dish/wakame"],["Yakgwa","dish/yakgwa"],["Yukpo","dish/yukpo"]]}]},"dish/italian-tomato-pie":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/italian-tomato-pie","n":"Italian tomato pie","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Italian-American cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Italian tomato pie is an Italian-American and Italian-Canadian baked good consisting of a thick, porous, focaccia-like dough covered with tomato sauce. It may be sprinkled with Romano cheese or oregano. It is not usually served straight from the oven, but allowed to cool and then consumed at room temperature or reheated. Like Sicilian pizza, tomato pie is baked in a large rectangular pan and usually served in square slices, although in Rhode Island it is cut into rectangular strips like pizza al taglio. Tomato pie descends from and resembles the Italian sfincione, although it is not the same dish. For instance, sfincione may have toppings, is usually served hot, and has a crust more like brioche than focaccia."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-italian-tomato-pie"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italian_tomato_pie"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Italian-American cuisine","cuisine/italian-american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Focaccia","ingredient/focaccia"],["Tomato sauce","ingredient/tomato-sauce"]]},{"h":"More from Italian-American cuisine","links":[["Buona","dish/buona"],["Chicken Francese","dish/chicken-francese"],["Dan Tana's","dish/dan-tana-s"],["Fettuccine Alfredo","dish/fettuccine-alfredo"],["Garlic bread","dish/garlic-bread"],["Italian beef","dish/italian-beef"],["Maggiano's Little Italy","dish/maggiano-s-little-italy"],["Rollatini","dish/rollatini"],["Spaghetti and meatballs","dish/spaghetti-and-meatballs"],["Stromboli (food)","dish/stromboli-food"],["Tetrazzini","dish/tetrazzini"],["Vodka sauce","dish/vodka-sauce"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/2/29/TomatoPie.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Frank DuRoss Jr. a.k.a. Fduross of English Wikipedia","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.5","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TomatoPie.jpg"}},"dish/kalitsounia":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/kalitsounia","n":"Kalitsounia","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Greek cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Kalitsounia are small cheese or herb snacks associated with the Greek island of Crete. They can be described as a treat, with different filling and serving variations. In the Chania region, salty kalitsounia are found, stuffed with the locally made mild whey cheese mizithra, or various herbs from the Cretan land (without cheese) and the sweet kalitsounia, also stuffed with mizithra but with honey poured on top. In the Lasithi region, they are mainly sweet, made with dough and filled with mizithra, cinnamon, and sometimes lemon zest."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-kalitsounia"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalitsounia"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Greek cuisine","cuisine/greek-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Honey","ingredient/honey"],["Mizithra","ingredient/mizithra"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Dimitra's Dishes","ref":"site/dimitrasdishes-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"dimitrasdishes.com","note":"Dimitra Alfieris's video channel and blog focused on classic Greek home cooking.","url":"https://dimitrasdishes.com","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from Greek cuisine","links":[["Alexis Restaurant","dish/alexis-restaurant"],["Avgolemono","dish/avgolemono"],["Baba ghanoush","dish/baba-ghanoush"],["Bouyiourdi","dish/bouyiourdi"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Dametra Cafe","dish/dametra-cafe"],["Diples","dish/diples"],["Fasolada","dish/fasolada"],["Freddo cappuccino","dish/freddo-cappuccino"],["Greek restaurant","dish/greek-restaurant"],["Greek salad","dish/greek-salad"],["İmam bayıldı","dish/imam-bay-ld"],["Kakavia (soup)","dish/kakavia-soup"],["Karydopita","dish/karydopita"],["Keşkek","dish/keskek"],["Ladera","dish/ladera"],["Noumboulo","dish/noumboulo"],["Pithari Taverna","dish/pithari-taverna"],["Psarosoupa","dish/psarosoupa"],["Spanakorizo","dish/spanakorizo"],["Taramasalata","dish/taramasalata"],["Tzatziki","dish/tzatziki"],["Yia Yia Mary's","dish/yia-yia-mary-s"]]}]},"dish/oyakodon":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/oyakodon","n":"Oyakodon","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Japanese cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Oyakodon, literally \"parent-and-child donburi\", is a donburi, or Japanese rice bowl dish, in which chicken, egg, sliced scallion (or sometimes regular onions), and other ingredients are all simmered together in a kind of soup that is made with soy sauce and stock, and then served on top of a large bowl of rice. The name of the dish is a poetic reflection of both chicken and egg being used in the dish."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-oyakodon"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oyakodon"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Just One Cookbook","ref":"site/justonecookbook-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"justonecookbook.com","note":"Namiko Chen's long-running blog and videos on Japanese home cooking.","url":"https://justonecookbook.com","suggested":true},{"name":"RecipeTin Japan","ref":"site/japan-recipetineats-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"japan.recipetineats.com","note":"Japanese home recipes from Yumiko, a Japan-born cook, s